--TEST--
`Core\Request::json()` answers the same document on every call and leaves the octets held, so `body()` and `post()` follow it in either order `rule:http-server/buffering-readers-share-the-body-and-streaming-readers-consume-it`
--POST_RAW--
{ "title" : "Q3 report" }
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A buffering reader keeps what it read, so the second call is not a second
// reading of the wire. A member that pulled again would find the wire drained
// and report a document the peer never sent; one that refused its own second
// call would raise a `LogicError` about a question with an answer in memory.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Request::json()), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Request::json()), "\n";

// What is held is the octets, not the decode: the spacing the peer wrote is
// still there for the reader that copies bytes out, and re-encoding the
// document is what dropped it above. The `--POST_RAW--` section's own closing
// newline is part of the body the peer sent, so this line writes no second one.
echo Core\Request::body();

// And the hold belongs to the request rather than to the member that filled
// it, so the reading that follows a `body()` is the same document again.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Request::json()), "\n";
--EXPECT--
{"title":"Q3 report"}
{"title":"Q3 report"}
{ "title" : "Q3 report" }
{"title":"Q3 report"}
